A||N||1||Spiff||3.4 Engine||||1||
Z||000000||Spiff||09-22-2001||04:03 PM||||I have the opportunity to get a 3.4 out of a wrecked (rear) Camaro. The guy want to sell the engine and tranny together, but I don't want the tranny (Auto). I want to keep my Getrag 5 speed. If I do get the engine, would anybody be interested in the tranny? Would I need the ECM and wiring harness from the Camaro? What can be done to the 3.4 to make as much reliable power as possible?<P>Any help would be useful.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||09-22-2001||04:16 PM||||Unfortunately, the tranny will be useless to any Fiero person, unless they also have a RWD GM car.<P>You do not need the wiring or the ECM from the Camaro.......your Fiero's harness and ECM will work just fine.  <P>Short of rebuilding it, there are a few bolt on things you can do to extract some more power.  First port out the factory exhaust manifolds (or buy a set of headers) and install a freer flowing exhaust system (cat & muffler).  Now you can also install an underdrive crank pulley (Power Pulley) to free up a couple of high RPM horses and along those same lines, get a bored out throttle body and upper plenum.  You can also port match the middle and lower intake manifolds too.<P>There are probably a few other things.....like performance coil/ignition stuff, performance plug wires, etc., but you get the idea......a good tune-up is a must.<P>If you were to rebuild it then the sky's the limit.  There are plenty of things in the archives if you go that route.<P>I plan on boring mine out at least 1mm and installing TDC pistons, balance it, big cam, etc., but it will be a long, drawn out process  that will get pretty expensive once done.  I guess it all depends on your budget and what you're willing to do.<P>Good luck!  <BR>          <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 09-22-2001).]||||reg||1
